%T A note on the hayek hypothesis and the favorite-longshot bias in parimutuel betting
%V 85
%N 4
%C Nashville, United States
%I American Economic Association
%P 949-955
%X The Hayek hypothesis (Vernon L. Smith, 1982) suggests that markets can work efficiently even when participants have a limited knowledge of the environment or of other participants. An examination is made of the so-called favorite-longshot bias in pari-mutuel betting (favorites win more often than the betting odds indicate, and longshots win less often than the betting odds indicate). It is suggested that the bias can be explained with an argument that turns on information and transactions costs (the track "take" is 15%-20% of win pools at most racetracks). A model is presented of win-pool pari-mutuel betting which posits 2 types of risk-neutral bettors: informed handicappers and uninformed handicappers. The results suggest that the favorite-longshot bias cannot be explained with an argument that appeals to information and transactions costs.
